workedExamples.question_text:
Use the vertical line test to decide whether this graph is a function. The upper ray ends with an open point at $(2,2)$; the lower ray begins with a closed point at $(2,-2)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<-] (-2.8,2)--(2,2);
\draw[curveblue,thick,->] (2,-2)--(3.8,-2);
\draw[curveblue,thick,fill=white] (2,2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill[curveblue] (2,-2) circle (2.5pt);
\draw[black] (2,-0.1)--(2,0.1);
\path (2,0) node[below,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
At $x=2$, the open point $(2,2)$ is excluded. Only the closed point $(2,-2)$ counts.

For $x<2$, a vertical line meets only the upper ray; for $x>2$, it meets only the lower ray. Every vertical line meets the graph once, so it is a function.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=8mm,y=8mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{testred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[black,->] (-3,0)--(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<-] (-2.8,2)--(2,2);
\draw[curveblue,thick,->] (2,-2)--(3.8,-2);
\draw[testred,dashed,thick] (2,-2.8)--(2,2.8);
\draw[curveblue,thick,fill=white] (2,2) circle (2.5pt);
\fill[testred] (2,-2) circle (2.5pt);
\path (2,2) node[above left] {excluded};
\path (2,-2) node[below left] {included};
\draw[black] (2,-0.1)--(2,0.1);
\path (2,0) node[below right,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

