This circle is not a function. Write down two points on it with the same $x$-coordinate, and explain how they show that it fails the vertical line test.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-4,0)--(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-4)--(0,4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick] (0,0) circle[radius=2.5];
\draw[black] (-0.1,2.5)--(0.1,2.5);
\draw[black] (-0.1,-2.5)--(0.1,-2.5);
\path (0,2.5) node[above left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$2.5$};
\path (0,-2.5) node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$-2.5$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

workedExamples.solution_text:
The points $(0,2.5)$ and $(0,-2.5)$ both have $x=0$ but different $y$-coordinates. The vertical line $x=0$ meets the circle twice, so the circle fails the test.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\definecolor{testred}{HTML}{EF6068}
\draw[black,->] (-4,0)--(4,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-4)--(0,4) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick] (0,0) circle[radius=2.5];
\draw[testred,dashed,thick] (0,-3.6)--(0,3.6);
\fill[testred] (0,2.5) circle (2.5pt);
\fill[testred] (0,-2.5) circle (2.5pt);
\path (0,2.5) node[above right] {$(0,2.5)$};
\path (0,-2.5) node[below right] {$(0,-2.5)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

For example, $x=-3$ is a vertical straight line. The vertical test line $x=-3$ coincides with it and meets it at infinitely many points.

One input, $-3$, has many outputs, so this line is not a function.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=7mm,y=7mm,every node/.style={text=black,font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont}]
\definecolor{curveblue}{HTML}{268CFF}
\draw[black,->] (-4.5,0)--(1.5,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[black,->] (0,-3)--(0,3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[curveblue,thick,<->] (-3,-2.7)--(-3,2.7);
\fill[curveblue] (-3,1.5) circle (2.5pt);
\fill[curveblue] (-3,-1.5) circle (2.5pt);
\path (-3,1.5) node[right] {$(-3,1.5)$};
\path (-3,-1.5) node[right] {$(-3,-1.5)$};
\draw[black] (-3,-0.1)--(-3,0.1);
\path (-3,0) node[below left,font=\fontsize{8.5}{10}\selectfont] {$-3$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

